In an arbitrary triangle, show that the line segment joining the midpoint of two sides is parallel to and has half the length of the third side. 
(see figure 1.44)
In other words, if M₁ is the midpoint os side AB and M₂ is the midpoint of side AC, we wish to show that M₁M₂ is parallel to BC and has half its length.

For a vector proof, we use the diagram in Figure 1.45, a slightly modified version of Figure 1.44. 
The midpoint conditions translate to the following statments about vectors: 

		AM₁-> = (1/2)AB->, 	AM₁-> = (1/2)AC->

Now, 

		M₁M₂-> = AM₂-> - AM₁-> = (1/2)AC-> - (1/2)AB-> = (1/2)(AC -> - AB->) = (1/2)BC->

But M₁M₂-> = (1/2)BC-> is precisely what we wish to prove: To say M₁M₂-> is a scalar times BC-> means that the two vector are parallel. 
Moreover, from part 1 of Proposition 3.4, 

		||M₁M₂->|| = ||(1/2)BC->|| = (1/2)||BC->||, 

so that the length condition also holds. 
